Superficial partial-thickness scald from spilled hot beverage to anterior thigh
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Downward trailing blister pattern, Moist blanching pink dermis, Hot liquid spill mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-034
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful scald burn requiring urgent assessment of total body surface area, depth estimation, and application of a sterile barrier dressing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute hot liquid spill resulting in immediate blistering and severe pain. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to calculate burn percentage, definitively rule out full-thickness injury, and apply specialized occlusive burn dressings to prevent secondary infection and treat severe unremitting pain prior to daytime clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I spilled freshly boiled tea on my lap; my thigh is bright red, blistering, and the burning pain is severe.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an accidental spill of a recently boiled liquid (tea) occurring 30 minutes ago. This resulted in an irregular patchy area of erythema and blistering with a distinct downward trailing or splash pattern on the anterior thigh. The patient experiences intense immediate burning pain localized exactly to the areas of liquid contact. Several fluid-filled vesicles have formed and ruptured to reveal a highly sensitive raw pink dermis. The severe pain is markedly exacerbated by clothing friction or exposure to room air.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently; holding clothing away from the skin.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor hazard in the kitchen regarding handling hot liquids; patient advised to use travel mugs for transport.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/86 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, in acute distress due to burn pain, visibly agitated when ambient air touches the wound.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals a burn area of approximately 2% Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) on the anterior thigh. There is a downward trailing blister pattern. The denuded skin remains highly moist and demonstrates a briskly blanching pink dermis upon applied tactile pressure. There is a total absence of full-thickness charring or coagulated painless white eschar. Intact sharp sensation to pinprick is evaluated within the center of the blistered areas. Systemic vital signs remain completely stable without any physiological evidence of burn shock.
Environmental Observations: Spilled liquid cleaned up; no ongoing safety hazard.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Patient appropriately ran cool water over the area for 20 minutes prior to calling.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial partial-thickness scald to anterior thigh - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Full-thickness (3rd degree) scald - Ruled out due to: Exquisite pain (intact nerves), brisk capillary refill, and moist, pink appearance lacking pale, leathery eschar.

Deep partial-thickness scald - Ruled out due to: Prompt capillary refill and bright pink dermis, rather than a sluggishly blanching, mottled pale appearance.

Chemical burn - Ruled out due to: Clear history of thermal hot liquid spill without exposure to caustic household chemicals.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Scald injuries from boiled liquids require meticulous clinical evaluation to rule out deep dermal destruction and accurately calculate TBSA to ensure outpatient management is appropriate. Bedside assessment confidently classified the injury as a superficial partial-thickness burn covering <10% TBSA without involving critical zones (face, hands, genitals). Urgent after-hours visitation was essential to halt the severe pain cycle with specialized occlusive dressings and provide sterile barrier protection, safely avoiding a delayed presentation or unnecessary transfer to a tertiary burn center.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Gently cleansed the wound with sterile saline. Ruptured blister remnants were debrided; intact blisters were preserved. Applied a specialized non-adherent burn dressing (e.g., silicone foam or hydrogel sheet) covered by a secondary retention layer. Tetanus status verified and up to date.
Positive Behavioral Change: Do not interfere with or open the sealed burn dressing for 3 days. Leaving the specialized dressing perfectly intact creates a moist, sterile micro-environment that rapidly suppresses the agonizing pain and encourages swift cellular healing without restrictive scarring.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Specialized burn dressings, tubular retention bandage.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or practice nurse review in 3 days for a complete wound inspection and dressing change.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if the burn suddenly becomes increasingly red, swollen, hot, starts leaking foul-smelling pus, or if you develop systemic fever, chills, or extreme lethargy.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Scald burns can evolve over the first 48 hours; strict adherence to dressing protocols is required to prevent infection.
Clinical Course: The sharp, burning pain will resolve almost entirely within a few hours under the sealed dressing. The burn will produce a large amount of clear/yellowish fluid over the first 3 days; this is normal. Healing takes 10 to 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the pain returns severely, if red lines track up your leg, or if you feel dizzy and confused.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The specialized dressing immediately blocks the air from hitting the exposed nerve endings, providing profound pain relief. It also absorbs the weeping fluid while keeping the wound bed safely moist.
Actionable Steps: Take prescribed paracetamol and ibuprofen regularly. Keep the thigh completely dry when showering. Do not attempt to peel the dressing off at home.

